## TL;DR: Ideal Emerald Cut Diamond Proportions

The best ratio for an emerald cut diamond is **L:W 1.30–1.50:1** for the shape, **60–64% depth**, and **61–69% table**. These three numbers define whether the step-cut facets produce the hall-of-mirrors effect or fall flat.

**The Proportion Sweet Spot (hit all three):**
- **L:W ratio:** 1.30:1 to 1.50:1 — classic elongation without stretching
- **Depth %:** 60–64% — maximum face-up size, no windowing
- **Table %:** 61–69% — wide enough for the hall-of-mirrors effect

**What kills the look:**
- Depth over 65% → The Depth Tax (paying for weight you can't see)
- Depth under 58% → The Windowing Effect (transparent holes in the table)
- L:W below 1.25:1 → looks square, loses the elongated character
- L:W above 1.60:1 → looks stretched, starts to resemble a baguette

**Quick answers:**
- How many facets: **57–58 facets** (step-cut on both crown and pavilion)
- Mixed cut? **No** — pure step cut on crown and pavilion
- Most important spec: **L:W ratio first, then depth %, then clarity**

→ **[Search emerald cut diamonds by proportion](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=emerald&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)**

---

## What Does "Best Ratio" Mean for an Emerald Cut Diamond?

The best ratio for an emerald cut diamond refers to the length-to-width ratio — the relationship between the stone's longest and shortest face-up dimensions. A 6.5mm × 4.5mm emerald cut has an L:W ratio of 1.44:1. A 6.5mm × 5.0mm emerald cut at the same carat weight has an L:W ratio of 1.30:1.

Same carat weight. Different shape impression.

I'm **Farzana Hasan**, a GIA-certified gemologist. I've evaluated [emerald cut diamonds](/emerald-cut-diamond) across every proportion range. The L:W ratio is the first number I check on any emerald cut — before price, before clarity, before color.

It determines what the stone looks like on a hand, and no other spec can fix a ratio that doesn't suit the buyer.

Full credentials and methodology at **[Diamond Critics](https://diamondcritics.com/about-farzana/)**.

The L:W ratio is one of three proportion numbers that define how an emerald cut performs. The other two are depth percentage and table percentage. These three together determine whether the step-cut facets create the hall-of-mirrors reflection pattern the emerald cut is known for — or whether the stone looks dull, glassy, or smaller than its carat weight suggests.

---

## The Three Proportions That Control Emerald Cut Performance

Three numbers control how an emerald cut diamond looks: L:W ratio, depth percentage, and table percentage. Each controls a different visual quality.

**L:W ratio** controls the shape of the stone's face-up outline. It determines whether the emerald cut reads as a classic rectangle, a near-square, or a dramatically elongated shape.

**Depth percentage** controls how much of the stone's carat weight is visible face-up. A stone with high depth hides weight in the pavilion below the setting. A stone with appropriate depth spreads that weight across the visible table.

**Table percentage** controls the size of the flat facet on top of the stone. Too large and the step-cut pattern loses depth. Too small and the hall-of-mirrors reflection narrows.

All three numbers appear on the GIA or IGI grading report and in the detailed specs of any certified stone listing. The grading report lists them as percentages — depth % is the total depth divided by the width, and table % is the table width divided by the total width.

---

## The L:W Ratio: What It Means and How to Choose

The length-to-width ratio is the most personal proportion decision in choosing an emerald cut. It doesn't have a "correct" answer — it has a personal answer based on how the buyer wants the stone to look on their hand.

**Below 1.25:1 — square territory.** An emerald cut at 1.20:1 or 1.25:1 looks square. The long sides and short sides are close to the same length. The rectangle character disappears. This is sometimes called a "square emerald cut" or confused with an Asscher cut (which is a square step cut with a specific facet pattern). Most buyers do not want this ratio for a classic emerald cut look.

**1.30:1 to 1.40:1 — classic emerald cut.** This is the most requested range. The stone reads clearly as a rectangle without being dramatically elongated. On most finger sizes, this ratio fills the finger width naturally. The step-cut facets create long reflections that sweep the full length of the stone without looking stretched.

**1.40:1 to 1.50:1 — modern elongated.** This range is increasingly popular. The longer rectangle creates a more visually dramatic stone. It elongates the appearance of the finger more than a 1.30:1 stone. At 1.45:1 or 1.50:1, the emerald cut takes on a sleek, architectural quality that photographs well and looks distinctive from rings with shorter ratios.

**Above 1.50:1 — true elongated territory.** Ratios above 1.50:1 push into the [elongated emerald cut](/elongated-emerald-cut-diamond) category. At 1.60:1 and above, the stone starts to look more like a baguette. Some buyers specifically want this look. Most buyers find it outside the classic emerald cut range.

> **Farzana says:** "The number I check first on any emerald cut is the L:W ratio. Below 1.30 it looks like a wide rectangle. Above 1.55 it starts to feel stretched. The sweet spot is 1.35 to 1.45 — elongated enough to read as elegant, not so long it loses the classic emerald cut character."

![Emerald cut diamond length-to-width ratio comparison from 1.25:1 to 1.55:1 showing shape differences](/images/emerald-cut-length-to-width-ratio-chart.avif)

