## Depth Percentage: The Hidden Size Killer

Depth percentage is the total depth of the stone (measured in mm from table to culet) divided by the width (the shorter dimension in mm). A 1ct emerald cut with dimensions 6.5mm × 4.5mm × 2.8mm has a depth of 2.8/4.5 = 62.2%.

The depth percentage controls how much of the stone's carat weight is visible from above. Every diamond is three-dimensional — length, width, and depth. The carat weight you pay for is the total mass of all three dimensions.

But when the stone is set in a ring, only the top is visible. The depth hides.

For a round brilliant cut, a slightly higher depth is acceptable because the round's facets are specifically designed to handle light at steep pavilion angles. The emerald cut is a step cut. Its flat, parallel facets are sensitive to pavilion angle in a way that brilliant facets are not.

An emerald cut at 67% depth is not just hiding weight — it's also degrading the optical performance of the step-cut facets, because the angle of the pavilion facets changes when depth increases.

**How to calculate depth percentage from a listing:** Most certified stone listings show depth as a percentage. If you have the raw millimeter measurements (length × width × depth), calculate it yourself: divide the depth mm by the width mm and multiply by 100. For a stone listed as 6.5mm × 4.5mm × 2.88mm: 2.88 ÷ 4.5 × 100 = 64.0%.

---

## The Depth Tax

The Depth Tax is the premium you pay for carat weight that is hiding in the pavilion of a deep emerald cut diamond.

When an emerald cut is cut at 67% or higher depth, the cutter has placed more material below the girdle than is optically useful. The extra depth does not improve the step-cut reflection pattern — it degrades it. The only thing it accomplishes is increasing the carat weight (and therefore the price) of the stone.

A 1ct emerald cut at 67% depth might measure only 6.1mm × 4.2mm face-up. A 1ct emerald cut at 61% depth measures approximately 6.5mm × 4.5mm face-up. Both are certified as 1 carat.

Both cost approximately the same because the carat weight is the same. But the 61% depth stone looks measurably larger and produces a better optical performance.

> **Farzana says:** "Every emerald cut buyer should run one quick calculation before they buy: divide the depth in mm by the width in mm. That's the depth percentage. If it's over 65%, you're paying for carat weight hidden in the pavilion. I call that the Depth Tax — and the buyer pays it while getting a smaller-looking stone."

The Depth Tax is not visible from the price. Two stones at the same carat, color, and clarity grade cost the same regardless of depth. But the 67% depth stone delivers less for that price.

Always calculate depth percentage before buying any certified emerald cut. The number is on the grading report.

![Emerald cut diamond depth and table percentage chart showing ideal ranges vs avoid zones](/images/emerald-cut-diamond-depth-table-percentage.avif)

---

## Table Percentage: The Reflection Width

Table percentage is the width of the flat top facet (the table) divided by the total width of the stone. A table of 65% means the table facet covers 65% of the stone's width, with the remaining 35% split between the crown facets on either side.

For an emerald cut, the table is the primary entry point for light. The step-cut facets on the crown direct light inward through the table and reflect it back up. A table that is too small (below 58%) narrows the entry point and reduces the volume of light flowing through the hall-of-mirrors pattern.

A table that is too large (above 72%) reduces the crown height, which flattens the step-cut pattern and makes the stone look glassy from the side.

The ideal emerald cut table is between 61% and 69%. Within this range, the stone's crown has enough height to create distinct step-cut facets while keeping the table wide enough to allow maximum light flow. The difference between a 62% table and a 67% table is not dramatic to the eye — both work well.

Outside the 58–72% range, the effect becomes clearly visible.

---

## The Windowing Effect

The Windowing Effect occurs when an emerald cut is cut too shallow — typically below 58% depth. At this depth, the pavilion angle flattens to the point where incoming light passes straight through the bottom of the stone rather than reflecting back up.

When you look straight down into a windowed emerald cut, you see a transparent zone in the center of the table — a "window" through which you can see your finger or the table surface below. The hall-of-mirrors reflection pattern disappears in this zone. The stone looks clear rather than reflective.

Windowing is the opposite problem from The Depth Tax. The Depth Tax hides weight below the girdle. The Windowing Effect places weight above the girdle at angles that fail to reflect light.

Both are proportion failures, but they are different failures in different directions. Avoiding both at the same time is exactly what the 60–64% depth range accomplishes.

Windowing cannot be corrected by better cut quality in the facets — it is a structural proportion failure. Any stone showing transparent windows at the center of the table is outside the acceptable proportion range. Do not buy it regardless of price.

---

## How Many Facets Does an Emerald Cut Diamond Have?

An emerald cut diamond has **57 facets** in the standard configuration, or **58 facets** if the stone has a small culet facet at the bottom point of the pavilion.

The 57 facets break down as follows:

<table class="dc-custom-table">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Section</th>
      <th>Facets</th>
      <th>Description</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Table</td>
      <td>1</td>
      <td>The large flat top facet</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Crown — corner facets</td>
      <td>4</td>
      <td>Triangular facets at the 4 corners of the crown</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Crown — step facets</td>
      <td>8</td>
      <td>Two rows of rectangular step facets on each long side (4 per side, 2 rows)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Crown — bezel facets</td>
      <td>4</td>
      <td>Trapezoidal facets between corner and step facets on the short sides</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Upper girdle facets</td>
      <td>8</td>
      <td>Thin facets immediately above the girdle</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Lower girdle facets</td>
      <td>8</td>
      <td>Thin facets immediately below the girdle</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Pavilion — corner facets</td>
      <td>4</td>
      <td>Triangular facets at the 4 corners of the pavilion</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Pavilion — step facets</td>
      <td>8</td>
      <td>Two rows of rectangular step facets on each long side of the pavilion</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Pavilion — main facets</td>
      <td>4</td>
      <td>The central pavilion facets on each side</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Culet</td>
      <td>0 or 1</td>
      <td>Small facet at the bottom point — present on older cuts, often absent on modern stones</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Total</strong></td>
      <td><strong>57–58</strong></td>
      <td></td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

The 57 facet count is the same as a standard round brilliant, but the arrangement is completely different. The round brilliant uses triangular and kite-shaped facets arranged for maximum light scatter. The emerald cut uses rectangular, parallel step facets arranged for the hall-of-mirrors reflection pattern.

Same count, completely different optical design.

Some emerald cuts from certain cutters have slightly different facet arrangements — 49 facets or 65 facets depending on how the cutter handles the corner and step rows. The 57–58 count is the standard most commonly referenced in GIA reports and industry descriptions.

---

## Is an Emerald Cut Diamond a Mixed Cut?

No. An emerald cut diamond is **not a mixed cut**. It is a pure step cut.

A mixed cut diamond uses two different cutting styles on different sections of the stone. The most common example is the Princess cut — its crown uses brilliant-style facets for maximum sparkle, while the pavilion uses step-style facets for a clean, architectural look. The Portuguese cut and some oval-with-stepped-crown designs are also mixed cuts.

The emerald cut uses step-cut facets on both the crown and the pavilion. The crown has rows of rectangular step facets descending from the table. The pavilion has rows of rectangular step facets ascending from the culet.

Both sections are step cuts.

This matters because the step-cut-on-both-sections design is what produces the hall-of-mirrors effect. If the pavilion used brilliant-style facets, the light scatter from the pavilion would break up the parallel step-cut reflection pattern from the crown. The emerald cut's optical character — slow, sweeping flashes of light — is only possible because both crown and pavilion use the same parallel step-cut geometry.

When you see an emerald cut described as a "step cut diamond," that is accurate. When you see it described as a "mixed cut," that is inaccurate unless the specific stone has an unusual facet modification.

---

## What Is Most Important in an Emerald Cut Diamond?

The most important spec in an emerald cut diamond is the **L:W ratio**, followed by **depth percentage**, followed by **clarity**. Color and carat weight follow after those three.

The L:W ratio matters first because it determines whether the stone looks like an emerald cut at all. A stone outside the 1.25–1.60 range has lost the characteristic rectangular elongation that defines this shape.

Depth percentage matters second because it controls face-up size and optical performance. A stone with ideal L:W but 67% depth looks smaller than it should and reflects light poorly through the step-cut pattern.

Clarity matters third — more than with any other diamond shape — because the emerald cut's open table and flat step-cut facets make inclusions more visible than in brilliant cuts. An SI1 that is "eye-clean" in a round brilliant can show visible inclusions in an emerald cut. VS2 is the practical starting point for clarity in an emerald cut; VS1 is the safer recommendation.

Color grade matters fourth. The step-cut facets reflect color more visibly than brilliant facets. In white gold or platinum, stay at G or above.

In yellow gold, H works well (see the [emerald cut diamond ring in yellow gold guide](/emerald-cut-diamond-ring-yellow-gold)).

Carat weight matters last among the five C's for an emerald cut — not because it's unimportant, but because an emerald cut at ideal proportions shows more face-up size than the same carat weight with poor proportions. Get the proportions right first, then choose a carat weight.

---

## What to Look For When Buying an Emerald Cut Diamond

When buying an emerald cut diamond, check these specifications in this order:

**Step 1 — Calculate L:W ratio.** Divide the listed length by the listed width. Target 1.30–1.50. This is personal preference within that range, but outside that range is a proportion failure.

**Step 2 — Check depth percentage.** The grading report lists it directly. Target 60–64%. Anything above 65% triggers The Depth Tax. Anything below 58% risks The Windowing Effect.

**Step 3 — Check table percentage.** Target 61–69%. This is listed on the grading report. Outside 58–72% is a concern.

**Step 4 — Verify polish and symmetry.** Both should be Very Good or Excellent. Good is acceptable. Fair or Poor means the cutter's workmanship was poor on the facet edges, which directly degrades the step-cut reflection pattern.

**Step 5 — Watch the clarity video.** For an emerald cut, always watch a high-resolution video of the specific stone before buying. Inclusions that are tolerable in a round brilliant can be very visible in an emerald cut's open table. An SI1 grade that looks clean in the video is buyable. An SI1 that shows a black carbon inclusion at the table center is not.

**Step 6 — Check the girdle thickness.** The girdle should be Thin to Medium. An extremely thin girdle is fragile — chipping risk at the corners. A Very Thick girdle hides carat weight in the edge, which is a smaller version of The Depth Tax.

**Step 7 — Look for a None or Very Small culet.** A large culet creates a visible circle at the center of the table when viewed face-up. Modern emerald cuts have no culet or a very small one.

![Emerald cut diamond buying checklist — 7-step proportion and quality verification guide](/images/emerald-cut-diamond-proportion-checklist.avif)

---

## Does Carat Weight Matter With Emerald Cut Diamonds?

Yes, carat weight matters with emerald cut diamonds — but proportion matters more. The same carat weight in two differently proportioned stones shows different face-up sizes, different optical performances, and different visual qualities.

A [1ct emerald cut](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=emerald&caratMin=0.9&caratMax=1.1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) at 61% depth and 1.40:1 L:W looks significantly larger and more impressive than a [1ct emerald cut](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=emerald&caratMin=0.9&caratMax=1.1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) at 68% depth and 1.25:1 L:W. The carat weight is the same. The price is approximately the same.

The visual result is very different.

Carat weight in emerald cuts also has a minimum threshold for the solitaire setting. The emerald cut's step-cut facets need sufficient surface area to produce the full hall-of-mirrors effect. Below approximately 0.75 carats, the step-cut facets are too small to produce the characteristic long reflections — the stone looks more like a small rectangle of light than the distinctive hall-of-mirrors pattern.

The minimum recommended carat weight for an emerald cut to fully express its character is **1 carat**.

For lab-grown options, carat weight becomes less expensive. A [lab-grown 2ct emerald cut](https://www.bluenile.com/lab-grown-diamond-search?shape=emerald&caratMin=1.9&caratMax=2.1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) at ideal proportions often costs less than a natural 1ct at the same quality grades. The proportions advice is identical for lab and natural stones — same L:W target, same depth target, same table target.

---

## How to Read Emerald Cut Proportions on a Certificate

The GIA grading report lists proportions in a dedicated section. The relevant numbers are:

**Table %** — Listed as a percentage. This is the table facet width as a percentage of the total stone width.

**Depth %** — Listed as a percentage. This is the total depth (culet to table) divided by the average width.

**Girdle** — Listed as a thickness range (e.g., "Thin to Medium"). Sometimes listed as a percentage range.

**Culet** — Listed as a size descriptor (None, Very Small, Small, Medium, etc.).

**Polish and Symmetry** — Each listed separately as Excellent, Very Good, Good, Fair, or Poor.

The L:W ratio is not listed directly on the GIA report. Calculate it from the measurements section, which gives the stone's length × width × depth in millimeters. Divide length by width to get the L:W ratio.

For an IGI report (common for lab-grown stones), the same proportion data appears in a similar layout. IGI uses the same terminology. The calculation method is identical.

---

## Expert Summary

The best ratio for an emerald cut diamond is a range, not a number. The L:W ratio of 1.30:1 to 1.50:1 defines the acceptable elongation window. The depth of 60–64% defines the face-up size window.

The table of 61–69% defines the reflection quality window.

The two proportion traps are The Depth Tax (stones above 65% depth that hide carat weight below the girdle) and The Windowing Effect (stones below 58% depth that create transparent holes in the table). Both are avoidable if you check the numbers before buying.

The emerald cut has 57–58 step-cut facets on a pure step-cut design — no brilliant facets, no mixed cutting. The step-cut design is what produces the hall-of-mirrors effect, and that effect is only produced when the proportions hit The Proportion Sweet Spot. Outside those proportions, the emerald cut's distinctive optical character degrades or disappears.

> **Farzana says:** "People ask me what matters most in an emerald cut. Every time: L:W ratio first, then depth percentage, then clarity grade — because the open table shows every inclusion. Color and carat come after those three. Get the first three right and the other two take care of themselves."

---

## Best For

**Buyers choosing between ratio options:** 1.35–1.45:1 is the classic choice. It reads as a clear rectangle on any finger size, looks balanced in photographs, and fits the widest range of ring settings. [Browse at this ratio range](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=emerald&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) and check listed dimensions.

**Buyers fighting The Depth Tax:** Filter diamond search results by minimum face-up dimensions. At 1ct, reject any stone showing under 6.3mm in length or 4.3mm in width — that signals depth above 65%. The [emerald cut diamond search](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=emerald&caratMin=0.9&caratMax=1.1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) lists dimensions for each stone.

**Buyers wanting maximum elongation:** Target 1.45–1.55:1 L:W. This gives a longer, slimmer look that elongates the finger more than the classic ratio. Stay within 60–64% depth. See the [elongated emerald cut guide](/elongated-emerald-cut-diamond) for the full elongated ratio analysis.

**Lab-grown buyers:** Same proportion targets apply. IGI-certified lab emerald cuts include full proportion data. A [2ct lab emerald cut](https://www.bluenile.com/lab-grown-diamond-search?shape=emerald&caratMin=1.9&caratMax=2.1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) at ideal proportions costs dramatically less than a natural equivalent. Run the same proportion checks — depth %, table %, L:W ratio — before buying any certified stone.

