## FAQs

### What is the best ratio for an emerald cut diamond?

The best L:W ratio for an emerald cut diamond is **1.30:1 to 1.50:1**. Within this range, 1.35:1 to 1.45:1 is the most popular for a balanced, classic emerald cut look. Below 1.25:1 the stone looks square.

Above 1.60:1 it looks stretched. Personal preference applies within the 1.30–1.50 range — both the shorter and longer ends of this range look excellent when the other proportions (depth and table) are also in range.

### What is a good depth for an emerald cut diamond?

A good depth for an emerald cut diamond is **60–64%** total depth percentage. Below 58% risks The Windowing Effect — transparent holes in the table where light passes through rather than reflecting. Above 65% triggers The Depth Tax — carat weight hidden in the pavilion that you cannot see.

The 60–64% range hits maximum face-up size and the best step-cut optical performance simultaneously.

### What is the ideal ratio for an emerald cut diamond?

The ideal ratio is **1.35:1 to 1.45:1** for most buyers who want the classic elongated emerald cut look. This range gives a clear rectangle that reads as longer than a round but not so long it looks stretched. Combined with 61–63% depth and 63–67% table, a stone in this ratio range hits The Proportion Sweet Spot across all three key measurements.

### How do I calculate the ratio of an emerald cut diamond?

Divide the length by the width. If a stone is listed as 6.5mm × 4.5mm, the ratio is 6.5 ÷ 4.5 = 1.44:1. The length and width are listed in the grading report measurements section.

The GIA report shows them as the first two of the three millimeter dimensions (length × width × depth). The L:W ratio does not appear directly on the report — you calculate it from the raw millimeter data.

### How many facets does an emerald cut diamond have?

An emerald cut diamond has **57 facets** in the standard configuration, or **58** if the stone has a small culet at the bottom point. The facets break into: 1 table, 16 crown facets (corner, bezel, and step), 16 girdle facets, and 24 pavilion facets (corner, main, and step). The total is the same as a standard round brilliant, but the arrangement is completely different — the emerald cut uses rectangular step facets arranged in parallel rows rather than triangular and kite-shaped brilliant facets.

### Is an emerald cut diamond a mixed cut?

No. An emerald cut is a **pure step cut**, not a mixed cut. Step-cut facets appear on both the crown and the pavilion.

A mixed cut uses brilliant facets on one section and step facets on another (the Princess cut is the most common example). The emerald cut's step-cut-on-both-sections design is specifically what produces the hall-of-mirrors optical effect — replacing either section with brilliant facets would destroy the characteristic slow, sweeping reflection pattern.

### What is most important when buying an emerald cut diamond?

The most important specification is the **L:W ratio**, followed by **depth percentage**, followed by **clarity**. The L:W ratio determines whether the stone looks like an emerald cut at all. Depth percentage determines whether you get the full face-up size for the carat weight you're paying for.

Clarity matters more for emerald cuts than for brilliant cuts because the open table shows inclusions that would be hidden by a brilliant cut's scatter. Color and carat weight come after those three.

### What does a good emerald cut diamond look like?

A good emerald cut diamond looks like a clean, clear rectangle with long, slow reflections sweeping across the table as the ring moves. The step-cut facets create a hall-of-mirrors effect — not the scattered sparkle of a brilliant, but deliberate, architectural flashes of white and dark. Under overhead light, a well-proportioned emerald cut shows alternating dark and light rectangular zones.

Under natural or diffuse light, it shows long, sweeping reflections. If the stone looks glassy and transparent, depth may be too shallow. If it looks smaller than the stated carat weight suggests, depth may be too high.

### What is a good size emerald cut diamond?

A good size for an emerald cut diamond depends on the setting and the buyer's hand, but the minimum recommended carat weight for the shape to fully express its hall-of-mirrors character is **1 carat** (approximately 6.5mm × 4.5mm). Below 1 carat, the step-cut facets are too small to produce long, sweeping reflections. At 1.5ct (7.5mm × 5.5mm), the stone fills the finger well in a solitaire.

At [2ct](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=emerald&caratMin=1.9&caratMax=2.1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) (8.5mm × 6.0mm) it is a clear statement stone.

### Does carat weight matter with emerald cut diamonds?

Yes, but proportions matter more. Two 1ct emerald cuts can look like different sizes depending on depth percentage and L:W ratio. The same carat weight at 61% depth looks significantly larger than at 68% depth.

Get the proportions right first — L:W 1.30–1.50, depth 60–64%, table 61–69% — then choose the carat weight that fits the budget. A 1ct with ideal proportions often looks better than a 1.25ct with poor proportions.

### What is the Windowing Effect in an emerald cut diamond?

The Windowing Effect occurs when an emerald cut is cut too shallow (below approximately 58% depth). The pavilion angle flattens to the point where incoming light passes straight through the stone rather than reflecting back up through the table. When you look face-down into the stone, you see a transparent zone — a "window" — where you see your finger or the surface beneath rather than the diamond's reflection.

A windowed emerald cut loses the hall-of-mirrors effect entirely in the affected zone. This is a proportion failure that cannot be corrected and makes the stone look cheap regardless of its color or clarity grade.

### What is the Depth Tax in an emerald cut diamond?

The Depth Tax is the premium you pay for carat weight hiding in the pavilion of a deep emerald cut. When depth exceeds 65%, the cutter has placed more material below the girdle than is optically useful. This adds carat weight — and therefore price — without adding face-up size or optical performance.

A 1ct emerald cut at 67% depth might measure only 6.1mm × 4.2mm face-up, while a 1ct at 61% depth measures 6.5mm × 4.5mm. Same certificate. Same carat.

Very different visual result. Always check depth percentage before buying.

### How does L:W ratio affect an emerald cut on the finger?

Higher L:W ratios (1.45–1.55) create a more elongated stone that visually lengthens the finger. This is the primary reason buyers choose higher ratios — the stone appears to stretch from knuckle to base rather than filling width. Lower L:W ratios (1.30–1.35) create a more compact rectangle that fills the finger more evenly.

On shorter fingers, higher ratios look proportionally elegant. On longer fingers, the classic 1.35–1.40 range often looks better balanced. Neither is objectively better — it depends on hand geometry and personal preference.

### How do I check emerald cut proportions before buying online?

Check the grading report for depth %, table %, polish, symmetry, girdle, and culet. Calculate the L:W ratio from the millimeter measurements in the report. Verify that depth is 60–64%, table is 61–69%, L:W is 1.30–1.50, polish and symmetry are Very Good or better, girdle is Thin to Medium, and culet is None or Very Small.

Then watch a high-resolution video of the specific stone to check for inclusions visible in the table. Reject any stone with depth above 65% (Depth Tax) or windowing visible in the video.

